Purpose & context
Dan is developing an interactive portfolio that demonstrates his unique competency in human-AI collaboration. Rather than simply showcasing completed projects, the portfolio aims to reveal "how Dan thinks about problems" - his ability to decompose complex challenges, iterate with AI systems, and ship real solutions. This core insight, which Dan calls "The Giant Orange Pushpin," represents a strategic breakthrough that reframed the entire approach from displaying finished work to demonstrating problem-solving methodology.
The portfolio uses a "Prism Model" to serve multiple audiences with the same core content, including technical leads, HR personnel, solutions architects, and forward-thinking managers. Each audience has different evaluation criteria and would traverse the content differently. Dan has three significant AI collaboration projects to feature as case studies: a RAG information retrieval system, a Catalan testing framework, and an n8n SMS business intelligence system.
Current state
Dan has established a comprehensive information architecture framework with three axes: Content Layers (Surface/Explanation/Evidence depth levels), Content Types (Methodology, Projects, Artifacts, Outcomes), and Audience Lenses. The project infrastructure is set up using Claude Desktop's MCP filesystem server for persistent, cross-session work with local file storage and version control integration.
The strategic foundation is complete, including detailed project documentation and a clear separation between content strategy and delivery mechanism. Dan is focusing on content structure first before addressing the technical implementation of the interactive portfolio.
Approach & patterns
Dan demonstrates sophisticated strategic thinking by treating AI collaboration like engaging consultants - using higher-capability models (Claude Opus) for strategic sessions and switching to more efficient models (Sonnet) for implementation work. He emphasizes meta-level thinking about approaches rather than diving directly into execution.
His workflow leverages persistent, version-controlled processes across multiple Claude projects and chat sessions. Rather than working with ephemeral chat artifacts, he uses MCP filesystem integration to write files directly to local directory structures, enabling true collaborative development with AI systems.
Dan validates strategic insights by translating them into concrete frameworks, ensuring that high-level concepts can be successfully operationalized into practical organizational structures.
Tools & resources
Dan uses Claude Desktop with MCP filesystem servers for persistent project work, storing files locally under a structured directory system in his repos folder. For Windows PowerShell execution policy restrictions, he runs unsigned scripts using "powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File scriptname.ps1" to avoid digital signing requirements.
The established workflow separates planning documents, content organized by type, and source code, with placeholder files and .gitkeep files to maintain empty directories in version control. This approach eliminates the limitations of ephemeral chat-based artifacts and enables true multi-session collaborative development.